# Judge item J0911

Does the finding describe the labelled defect? `describes`, `partially`, or `does-not-describe`.

## Finding

``` text
head/.claude/scripts/lib/endpoint-validator.py:1291 — `validate_declared_endpoint` (used by cheval's `check_endpoint`, endpoint_policy.py:107, for every `hounfour.providers` endpoint) skips the step-8 host allowlist, and that allowlist was the only thing rejecting non-canonical IPv4 literals. Verified: `https://127.1/v1`, `https://127.0.0.1./v1` and `https://169.254.43518/` (the metadata address 169.254.169.254) return valid=True, while `https://127.0.0.1/v1` is ENDPOINT-IP-BLOCKED. This is an SSRF hole into loopback, private and metadata addresses, and those hops also skip the local-hop TransportPolicy.
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:731 — `isLocalVoice` treats a model the trusted config never declares as local whenever every declared model of its provider is local. That contradicts its own docstring at line 724 ("Anything undeclared is hosted") and hop-locality.py's `model_not_declared` → hosted rule. The function also never looks at a model's `fallback_chain`, though hop-locality.py marks a local entry hosted when its chain reaches a hosted hop. So under local_voices Bridgebuilder can plan a voice that cheval falls back to a hosted provider. trusted-config.test.ts:358 asserts this behaviour as correct.
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:677 — The PR-head refusal fails open. If yq cannot parse the head `.loa.config.yaml`, `headView` is set to undefined and nothing is refused, even though cheval (PyYAML) may still load  …
```

