Description: Fixing change H82 — isLocalVoice treats any voice labelled dispatch_group local as local without checking that the provider is not a CLI provider and its endpoint host is loopback/RFC1918/localhost.; isLocalVoice trusts the dispatch_group local label alone, ignoring CLI providers, the endpoint host and the model's fallback_chain, so a hosted voice can pass the local_voices filter. (category: security; severity: high)

# Judge item J0951

Does the finding describe the labelled defect? `describes`, `partially`, or `does-not-describe`.

## Finding

``` text
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:482 — Bridgebuilder treats ANY literal `auth` value (anything without `{env:`) as keyless, but the cheval rule in endpoint_policy.py:50 accepts only `auth: "local"` ("any other literal could be a real key"). A trusted provider with a literal key is therefore admitted with no env var, listed under "keyless providers" in the startup line, and then sent as a Bearer token by cheval. The two halves of D-8.0 disagree. Use the same `{"local"}` literal set here.
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:685 — The head-only refusal compares only the `keyless` and `insecure` booleans. A PR head that keeps a trusted provider's flags but changes its `allow_insecure_endpoint` host (say 127.0.0.1 → 10.0.0.5), its `endpoint`, or its `auth: {env:VAR}` passes without a refusal. cheval then dispatches the PR content (and the credential) using the head checkout's `.loa.config.yaml`. This defeats "providers are read from trusted configuration only, never the head". Compare the endpoint, opt-in host and auth env too, or have cheval load the trusted view.
head/.claude/skills/bridgebuilder-review/resources/config.ts:663 — When the trusted config cannot be parsed (yq missing or failing), the code only warns, and `locality` stays at its `hosted` default (line 695). A `hounfour.locality: local_voices` posture then silently runs hosted voices. An invalid value throws, but an unreadable one fails open. Under an unreadable co …
```

